One thing that stands out about the diegetic music of _Stray_ is the diegetic
means by which much of it is presented to the viewer. One example of this
occurence is the music which can be heard playing from various radios
positioned throughout the world. The music that comes out of the radio is
played with a somewhat "tinny" or distorted sound, as though played or recorded
on imperfect speakers. Additionally, the sound changes dynamically as the
player moves around, giving the impression that the player is really moving
around as the direction, sharpness, and volume of the sound changes to reflect
the player's position relative to the speaker. In other words, you hear the
sounds of the radio music as though you're hearing it through the ears of the
cat you play as.

This design choice increases immersion and makes players feel like they are
really existing in the world, rather than just looking in. Munday's definition
of immersion, which states that it describes the sensation of being transported
into a mediated alternate reality, is a rather accurate description of this
phenomenon @mivg. Of course, one doesn't truly feel as though they've been
literally transported into the game's world just by hearing the sound of the
radio believeably change when moving around, but it perhaps allows them to
forget that they aren't somewhere else and focus only on what the game is
showing them.

The audio spatial distortion isn't the only thing that makes the radios add to
the immersive experience, for they share another major similarity to real world
radios: the music itself. Radios in the real world play popular songs that
people like listening to, and the same is true in the game world. Based on the
selections that appear on the radio and reappear in different ways throughout
the game (which will be discussed at length later in this paper), we can assert
that the robots have developed a unique musical culture, distinct from the
human music that seemingly served as its foundation.

The radio selections include several different songs, which vary widely in
their forms. There are some songs that resemble jazz music, some similar to
hip-hop music, and several other genres. Additionally, alongside what we humans
would consider music, there plays on the radio a track or two that is not
conventional music by any means, but rather an array of seemingly random notes
and tones, but the robots don't seem to notice this difference, instead acting
as if it's just as pleasant to listen to as any other track.

The radio, then, functions as a kind of cultural mirror, reflecting back to the
player a society that has developed its own aesthetic sensibilities and
preferences. But passive listening is only one dimension of musical culture. In
human societies, music is not merely consumed but also actively practiced,
preserved, and passed down through performance and the study of existing works.
The robots of _Stray_ are no different in this regard, and the game makes this
dimension of their culture equally tangible through the ways in which music is
not just heard, but played.
